{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":108144,"items":[{"DOI":"10.1007\/s00026-010-0049-2","title":["Generating Functions for Permutations Avoiding a Consecutive Pattern"],"author":[{"given":"Jeffrey","family":"Liese","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jeffrey","family":"Remmel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2010,2,16]]},"container-title":["Annals of Combinatorics"],"type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":4,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s00026-010-0049-2"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-642-17333-2_4","title":["Bijections between 321- and 132-avoiding permutations"],"author":[{"given":"Sergey","family":"Kitaev","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2011]]},"container-title":["Monographs in Theoretical Computer Science. An EATCS Series","Patterns in Permutations and Words"],"type":"book-chapter","is-referenced-by-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-642-17333-2_4"},{"abstract":"<jats:p xml:lang=\"en\">We prove that the number of permutations which avoid 132-patterns and have exactly one 123-pattern, equals $(n-2)2^{n-3}$, for $n \\ge 3$. We then give a bijection onto the set of permutations which avoid 123-patterns and have exactly one 132-pattern. Finally, we show that the number of permutations which contain exactly one 123-pattern and exactly one 132-pattern is $(n-3)(n-4)2^{n-5}$, for $n \\ge 5$.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.46298\/dmtcs.261","type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Permutations Containing and Avoiding $\\textit{123}$ and $\\textit{132}$ Patterns"],"author":[{"given":"Aaron","family":"Robertson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Mathematics [Hamilton NY]"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"container-title":["Discrete Mathematics &amp; Theoretical Computer Science"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[1999,1,1]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.46298\/dmtcs.261"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/cbo9780511902499.010","title":["Enumeration schemes for words avoiding permutations"],"author":[{"given":"Lara","family":"Pudwell","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2010,6,3]]},"container-title":["Permutation Patterns"],"type":"book-chapter","is-referenced-by-count":5,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/cbo9780511902499.010"},{"abstract":"<jats:p xml:lang=\"en\">Given permutations \u03c3 of size k and \u03c0 of size n with k &lt; n, the permutation pattern matching problem is to decide whether \u03c3 occurs in \u03c0 as an order-isomorphic subsequence. We give a linear-time algorithm in case both \u03c0 and \u03c3 avoid the two size-3 permutations 213 and 231. For the special case where only \u03c3 avoids 213 and 231, we present a O(max(kn 2 , n 2 log log n)-time algorithm. We extend our research to bivincular patterns that avoid 213 and 231 and present a O(kn 4)-time algorithm. Finally we look at the related problem of the longest subsequence which avoids 213 and 231.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.46298\/dmtcs.1329","type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":3,"title":["Permutation Pattern matching in (213, 231)-avoiding permutations"],"author":[{"given":"Both","family":"Neou","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Laboratoire d'Informatique Gaspard-Monge"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2387-0952","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Romeo","family":"Rizzi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science [Verona]"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2308-6970","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"St\u00e9phane","family":"Vialette","sequence":"additi